//? Title: Wasserstein Autoencoder: Deterministic Codes and Aggregate MMD
//? Subtitle: Pixel-summed squared cost, signed unbiased kernels and explicit prior samples
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A deterministic encoder can be regularized by comparing a batch of codes with an independent
prior sample. This report fits a compact WAE-MMD on 210 binary shapes, with separate pools
of 42 validation and 42 test images. All 21 Adam steps, Gaussian banks and checkpoint criteria
remain visible in C++. Epoch 3 minimizes validation reconstruction cost. Test MSE 0.103036
is worse than the fitting-only mean's 0.048548; validation MMD rises during fitting. The
unfiltered reconstructions and prior outputs remain visible alongside these limitations.
)
\toc

> Reconstruction and aggregate matching take different paths

[Tolstikhin, Bousquet, Gelly and Schölkopf (2018)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.01558)
express transport through a latent encoder constrained to reproduce the prior marginal,
then relax that constraint with a discrepancy penalty. WAE-MMD compares aggregate encoded
data with prior samples, rather than applying a Gaussian KL to each input. This report uses
a deterministic encoder and fixed averaged IMQ kernels in a small teaching architecture.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/wae_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram          d(51, 1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle st;
  st.width     = 5.4;
  st.height    = 2.6;
  st.font_size = 19;
  d.node("x", {0, 0}, "Image pixels\n784 values", st)
      .node("e", {7, 0}, "Tanh encoder 16\nLinear code 4", st)
      .node("z", {14, 0}, "Deterministic codes\nOne row per image", st)
      .node("g", {21, 0}, "Tanh decoder 16\nLinear 784 / sigmoid", st)
      .node("r", {28, 0}, "Squared pixel sums\nMean over images", st)
      .node("p", {14, -6}, "Explicit prior sample\nIndependent N(0,I)", st)
      .node("m", {21, -6}, "Signed unbiased MMD\nIMQ scales 1,4,16", st)
      .node("l", {28, -6}, "Objective\nReconstruction +10 MMD", st)
      .edge("x", "e")
      .edge("e", "z")
      .edge("z", "g")
      .edge("g", "r")
      .edge("z", "m")
      .edge("p", "m")
      .edge("m", "l")
      .edge("r", "l");
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke target;
  target.arrow = true;
  target.color = {100, 116, 139};
  d.path({{0, 1.3}, {0, 3.5}, {28, 3.5}, {28, 1.3}}, target)
      .text({14, 4.2}, "Original image target / squared reconstruction cost", 19);
  d.text(
       {14, -11},
       "Prior rows enter only the aggregate regularizer.\nCodes and reconstructions do not depend "
       "on those draws.\nThe caller owns Gaussian banks, batches, reverse mode and Adam.",
       20)
      .save_svg("build/wae_method.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/wae_method.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The prior sample joins the encoded batch at MMD. Generation instead supplies a prior code directly to the shared decoder; no image or encoder is needed.)
\alttext(Images pass through deterministic encoding and decoding into squared reconstruction cost. A separate prior sample meets encoded codes at MMD, and the two losses join in the explicit objective.)

Writing z=E(x), reconstruction r=G(z) and independent prior rows u, the actual objective is

$$
L=\frac1B\sum_i\sum_{j=1}^{784}(x_{ij}-r_{ij})^2+10\widehat{\operatorname{MMD}}_u^2(z,u),\\
k(a,b)=\frac13\sum_{c\in\{1,4,16\}}\frac{c}{c+\|a-b\|^2},\\
\widehat{\operatorname{MMD}}_u^2=
\frac{\sum_{i\ne j}k(z_i,z_j)}{B(B-1)}+
\frac{\sum_{i\ne j}k(u_i,u_j)}{P(P-1)}-
\frac{2\sum_{i,j}k(z_i,u_j)}{BP}.
$$
\alttext(The fitted loss averages pixel-summed squared reconstruction errors and adds ten times the signed unbiased two-sample MMD squared. Three IMQ scales are averaged, within-sample diagonals are excluded and every cross pair is included.)

Both sample counts must be at least two, but may differ. The unbiased finite-sample
estimate can be negative; no square root or clipping is applied. Its unbiasedness assumes
independent samples. Self-comparing one finite array is a useful algebraic control, not an
independent two-sample experiment. The sigmoid supplies bounded decoder values; it does not
turn squared-error fitting into a Bernoulli likelihood. There is no posterior noise, local
KL, discriminator or image-density estimate.

> A two-by-three example exposes every normalization

Take z=[0,1] and u=[0,2,3]. Self-kernels have unit diagonals, so the within-z sum drops two
entries and divides by two; the within-u sum drops three and divides by six. The cross
matrix keeps all six entries, including the matching zero pair. All matrices and scalar
terms below come from the native API.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/wae_kernel_pairs.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n  = dm::nn;
  const auto t = n::DType::Float64;
  const auto z = n::tensor({0., 1.}, {2, 1}, t), u = n::tensor({0., 2., 3.}, {3, 1}, t);
  const auto m = n::models::ImqMmd(z, u);
  std::print(
      "| term | value |\n|---|---:|\n| within z | {:.9f} |\n| within u | {:.9f} |\n| cross mean | "
      "{:.9f} |\n| signed MMD squared | {:.9f} |\n| same-array z control | {:.9f} |\n",
      m.within_x.item(),
      m.within_y.item(),
      m.cross.item(),
      m.value.item(),
      n::models::ImqMmd(z, z).value.item());
  dm::graphics::Diagram          d(41, 1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle st;
  st.width      = 2.8;
  st.height     = 1.6;
  st.font_size  = 17;
  auto excluded = st;
  excluded.fill = dm::RGB{254, 226, 226};
  auto kept     = st;
  kept.fill     = dm::RGB{219, 234, 254};
  d.text({1.6, 4}, "Within z / exclude diagonal", 21);
  for (int i = 0; i < 2; ++i)
    for (int j = 0; j < 2; ++j)
      d.node(
          "zz" + std::to_string(i * 2 + j),
          {3.3 * j, 1.8 - 2 * i},
          i == j ? "Excluded self" : std::format("{:.6f}", m.xx.at({i, j})),
          i == j ? excluded : kept);
  d.text({13, 4}, "Cross z,u / keep all six", 21);
  for (int i = 0; i < 2; ++i)
    for (int j = 0; j < 3; ++j)
      d.node(
          "zu" + std::to_string(i * 3 + j),
          {9.7 + 3.3 * j, 1.8 - 2 * i},
          std::format("{:.6f}", m.xy.at({i, j})),
          kept);
  d.text(
       {8, -5},
       "Within z divides by 2; within u divides by 6; cross mean divides by 6.\nUnit self-kernels "
       "are omitted only from the two within-sample sums.",
       20)
      .save_svg("build/wae_kernel_pairs.svg");
}
```
