\image(figures/wae_priors.png)
// result_end

\caption(All twenty unfiltered standard-normal prior decoder outputs. Weak or ambiguous shapes remain visible; this short fit does not establish generation quality.)
\alttext(Twenty decoder images generated from explicit standard-normal codes are displayed five per row, without selection.)

> Evidence and limits

At fixed selected weights, changing the prior changes the aggregate discrepancy but leaves
codes and reconstructions bit-exact. Unequal image/prior counts preserve reconstructed rows;
singleton samples fail before the unbiased denominator is used. Repeated evaluation and
code-only decoding agree, and all eight saved leaves remain unchanged during inference.
Those are mechanism checks, not likelihood or optimal-transport certificates.

Dense work is O(BH(F+L)); IMQ work/storage includes O((B²+P²+BP)L) because pairwise differences
are materialized. Validation on the whole 42-row pool uses its own full-pool discrepancy;
it is not an average of minibatch MMD estimates. Prior independence and data-split provenance
are explicit caller responsibilities. Fixed scales 1,4,16 are averaged rather than summed
or fitted; changing normalization changes the effective coefficient 10.

The optional independent NumPy development oracle checks all 26,036 first derivatives, the
exact first Adam step, 52 saved endpoint fields/658,778 values, 3,776 Gaussian values, signed
MMD/translation controls, all three eligible validation criteria, 630 batch rows and all
twenty prior predictions. It neither fits nor prepares report data:

```
python3 scripts/check_wasserstein_autoencoder.py docs/mepml/examples/nn/2_autoencoders/2.2_variational_autoencoders/build/wae
```

This compact run demonstrates an explicit aggregate regularizer and deterministic code-only
decoding. Reconstruction improves across these checkpoints but remains worse than the fixed
mean baseline; MMD worsens. More updates or altered hyperparameters would be a separate
experiment, not a reason to suppress these results.
